\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://milvus.io/docs/overview.md"}}{\fldrslt 
\fs28 \cf2 \ul \ulc2 Milvus docs}}
\fs28 \cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\fs40 \cf3 Here are some of the features of Milvus:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Milvus uses a distributed architecture that separates storage and computing, allowing for horizontal scalability in computing nodes.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Milvus can be scaled to handle trillions of vectors and millions of queries per second.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Milvus supports various data types, and it provides enhanced vector similarity search with attribute filtering, UDF support, configurable consistency level, time travel, and more\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Milvus can handle high query throughput and low latency searches.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
To help users try Milvus quicker, Bin Ji, a top contributor to the Milvus community, developed\'a0{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://milvus.io/docs/milvus_lite.md"}}{\fldrslt \ul Milvus Lite}}, a lightweight version of Milvus. It can help you get started with Milvus in minutes, while at the same time offering many benefits.\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Milvus provides a user-friendly interface for managing vectors and performing searches.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs48 \cf3 How To Choose The Right Vector Database For Your LLM Projects\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\b0\fs40 \cf3 To choose the right vector database for LLM projects, there are some factors you should consider. They include:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f5\b \cf3 Scalability
\f4\b0 : Since LLMs generate and consume vast amounts of vector data, it is best to choose a database that can efficiently store and manage large-scale datasets without compromising performance. Also, the vector database must be able to seamlessly handle future data additions and expansion of your LLM project\'92s scope.\

\f5\b Performance:\'a0
\f4\b0 It should deliver fast query execution and swift retrieval of relevant vectors. It should also efficiently handle multi-dimensional queries and complex similarity searches.\

\f5\b Security:\'a0
\f4\b0 The database should provide robust security features, including encryption, access controls, and authentication mechanisms. For use cases with personal or sensitive data, the vector database should align with applicable privacy regulations.\

\f5\b Cost:\'a0
\f4\b0 Using LLM APIs already costs a fortune when running at scale, so you look out for a vector base. with flexible pricing models and one that fits your use case.\

\f5\b Query interfaces
\f4\b0 : Evaluate the ease of interaction with the database, including available query languages, APIs, and user interfaces.\

\f5\b Deployment options:
\f4\b0 \'a0Make sure the vector database whether cloud-based, on-premise, or hybrid solutions matches your infrastructure preferences and data sensitivity.\

\f5\b Integration capabilities
\f4\b0 : Ensure seamless integration with your existing LLM infrastructure and other tools in your workflow.\
Yep! That\'92s it.\
Vector databases are generally useful when building LLM applications because they serve as an abstraction layer for handling and managing LLM data.\
